WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.420
Welcome to This Explainer. Today, we're tackling a challenge that honestly plagues product teams everywhere. I'm talking about the absolute headache of transforming a chaotic, ever-growing backlog, you know, the one keeping you up at night, into a strategic, flexible roadmap. Think of this as a hands-on tutorial. We're going to unpack the exact tools and frameworks you need to build a roadmap that actually works. We'll use real data, objective frameworks, and a structure that embraces the totally unpredictable nature of business, rather than fighting against it.

2
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.040
Here's our game plan for today.

3
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.660
1. Outcomes vs. Outputs

4
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.780
2. Prioritization Models

5
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.060
3. The Art of Saying No

6
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.400
4. Tech Debt as ROI

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.660
and 5. Building the Now, Next, Later Map

8
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.060
Let's dive right in.

9
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.720
Alright, Section 1. Outcomes vs. Outputs

10
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.600
This is a total paradigm shift.

11
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.020
A successful roadmap is really a strategic communication tool

12
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.600
that explains the where and why of your product's direction.

13
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:03.000
It is absolutely not a giant Gantt chart of broken promises detailing exactly who is doing what on some random Tuesday.

14
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.120
You've got to make this mental shift right out of the gate.

15
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:13.780
When you frame a roadmap item as an output, something like build a new onboarding feature, you essentially lock your team into a predetermined solution.

16
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:23.560
But when you reframe it as a measurable outcome, say, improve activation by 15%, you give your team the total freedom to find the absolute best path forward.

17
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.460
I mean, sometimes a winning solution to boost activation is just a better automated email campaign.

18
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.920
No code required at all.

19
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.440
Moving right along to section two, prioritization models.

20
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.220
This is about turning chaos into order.

21
00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:42.940
Look, you probably have something like 200 items in your backlog, but only one development team.

22
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.420
The CEO wants AI, sales wants integrations, and support is literally begging for bug fixes.

23
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.800
We have to objectively filter this chaos down to your top 10 priorities.

24
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.680
Now, there is no single perfect prioritization model out there.

25
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.220
You have to select the right framework based on your project's current level of business uncertainty.

26
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:09.420
For example, if you need to deeply understand user expectations, Kano is fantastic for separating basic must-haves from delighters.

27
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.800
Need to quickly align internal stakeholders?

28
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.340
Moscow categorizes things into really clear boundaries of must, should, could, and won't have.

29
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.940
Or if you're running fast-growth experiments with a lot of high uncertainty, ICE is definitely your go-to.

30
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.780
But for our core tutorial today, where we're looking to build a quarterly roadmap,

31
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.480
we're going to apply the RICE model to objectively score and rank our product initiatives.

32
00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.260
RICE stands for Reach, Impact, Confidence, and Effort.

33
00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.580
You basically multiply reach by impact and confidence, and then divide that whole thing by the effort,

34
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.380
meaning the number of weeks it takes one person to build it.

35
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:54.960
By quantifying an impact score on a set scale, say 0.25 for minimal up to 3 for massive, and demanding a realistic confidence percentage, this model literally takes the emotion out of the room.

36
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.360
It turns a shouting match into a structured, rational conversation about assumptions.

37
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:07.720
So the crucial takeaway here is how you use these numbers to justify your choices, especially when you're looking at the cost of delay.

38
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:14.700
Imagine a stakeholder is demanding a custom reporting feature, but you've prioritized single sign-on, or SSO.

39
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:21.520
Well, if missing that SSO integration means a cost of delay of $150,000 in lost enterprise deals every single month,

40
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.900
clear data effortlessly wins the argument over gut feelings.

41
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:30.360
Which brings us to Section 3, the incredibly important art of saying no.

42
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:36.560
Now that we have our prioritized data-backed list, we have to face what's probably the hardest part of product management,

43
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.700
actually rejecting stakeholder requests. Let's look at how to handle this.

44
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.720
Steve Jobs hit the nail on the head when he said innovation is saying no to a thousand things.

45
00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.860
True prioritization actually hurts. If your decisions aren't making anyone even a little

46
00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.620
bit uncomfortable, you honestly haven't prioritized. You've just made a wish list.

47
00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.480
Every single time you say yes to one feature, you are silently saying no to something else

48
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.440
because your team's capacity is totally finite. So here are four proven strategies for negotiating

49
00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:11.840
those trade-offs when rejecting feature requests without burning any bridges. First, present the

50
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.320
trade-off explicitly. Hey, we can build this, but it delays the onboarding revamp we already

51
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.780
committed to. Second, return to the strategy. Our quarterly OKR is retention, but this request

52
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:27.680
serves acquisition. Third, use the data. Point out that a requested tweak only impacts 3% of users

53
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.840
while a current bug is impacting 40%. And finally, offer an alternative. Instead of building a massive

54
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.180
of custom integration right now,

55
00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.260
offer a simple CSV export workaround.

56
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.640
It might just solve 80% of the customer's problem

57
00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:40.900
in a fraction of the time.

58
00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.220
Okay, section four,

59
00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.160
technical debt as a business investment.

60
00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:46.940
This is another really vital area of the roadmap

61
00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.680
that constantly gets overlooked.

62
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:50.920
While we manage external requests,

63
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.720
we absolutely have to protect the internal health

64
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.840
of our product by addressing technical debt.

65
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.700
The biggest mistake product managers make here

66
00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.720
is letting engineers present tech debt

67
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.380
as just a technical complaint.

68
00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:09.160
To secure a budget for necessary engineering maintenance, you must translate that technical debt into compelling business ROI.

69
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:14.000
For example, if an aging database is slowing your team down, frame it like this.

70
00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:19.800
A four-week database migration will save 30% of development time on every single feature next quarter.

71
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.940
Suddenly, that's not a technical chore. That's a high-yield business investment.

72
00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:27.400
And here is the golden rule for maintaining your momentum.

73
00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.960
You must incorporate 15-20% of every single sprint for managing long-term infrastructure health.

74
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:39.180
Do not wait around for a mythical tech debt month, because trust me, it will never get approved.

75
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.980
Bake it right into your continuous cycles so your ability to innovate doesn't completely grind to a halt under the weight of old code.

76
00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:48.760
Finally, Section 5, Building the Map.

77
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:50.420
Now, next, later.

78
00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.860
We've got our prioritized initiatives, our stakeholder buy-in, and our tech debt budget totally secured.

79
00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.220
Let's actually assemble the roadmap itself.

80
00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.560
Forget strict, rigid dates.

81
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:01.720
They're a trap.

82
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.420
One delayed feature causes customers to threaten to leave and sales to absolutely panic.

83
00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.720
Instead, map your prioritized outcomes across three time horizons.

84
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.700
Now, next, and later.

85
00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.220
This elegantly respects the inherent uncertainty of your business

86
00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.160
while still providing total, crystal-clear direction.

87
00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.160
So what's now?

88
00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:26.660
This represents work happening immediately, usually the next two to four weeks, perfectly

89
00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.760
aligning with standard development sprints.

90
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.500
Because it's happening right now, it has incredibly high certainty.

91
00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.580
This is where you have granular detail, committed user stories, assigned developers, and clear-cut

92
00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.680
metrics for success.

93
00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.200
Then we have the next horizon.

94
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.260
Think roughly one to three months out.

95
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.700
This is where we show our strategic intent for the upcoming quarter.

96
00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:55.480
We know the broad themes are epics, you know, those large bodies of work we want to tackle, but the exact features and user stories remain totally flexible.

97
00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:59.700
This lets us privet the details based on the data and feedback we're learning right now.

98
00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:04.400
And later, that covers three to six months out or even further.

99
00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.660
This acknowledges a very high level of uncertainty.

100
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.260
It essentially functions as an indicator of future exploration.

101
00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:15.380
Things like explore AI features or public APIs.

102
00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.680
It's a strategic intention and explicitly not a binding promise to your stakeholders or your sales team.

103
00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.020
Okay, let's quickly summarize your masterclass checklist.

104
00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:27.280
Frame outcomes over outputs.

105
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.020
Filter your top 10 initiatives using the RICE model.

106
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.220
Protect 20% of your sprint capacity for tech debt.

107
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.780
Map everything into now, next, and later horizons.

108
00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.620
And finally, negotiate buy-in using hard data.

109
00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.480
By doing this, you transform from merely managing a backlog to actively driving your product's strategic direction.

110
00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:51.360
I want to leave you with a final, slightly provocative thought as you take these tools back to your teams.

111
00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:57.340
If your roadmap doesn't disappoint at least one important stakeholder, are you really prioritizing?

112
00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:03.060
Remember, a great roadmap is a story of your product's future, and it's built on hard choices.

113
00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.880
So use these frameworks to make those choices count.

114
00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.040
Thanks for joining me on this explainer, and I'll catch you next time.

